      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
AIR SEALING

Overall, your house is of reasonably tight construction from an air leakage perspective, but I have identified a few locations that could be addressed:

** The most obvious source is in the furnace room on the lowest floor. During the blower door test I discovered a lot of cold air being pulled in around the outside of the furnace ducting, between the ducts and the ceiling drywall. Perhaps, cold air is being pulled in through some inaccessible attachment with the attic, or elsewhere that was unseen. Regardless of the actual outdoor location, you could attempt to solve this problem by sealing all ceiling penetrations in your mechanical room. Around the ducting this could be done with spray foam. Based on the location of this ducting I can say with reasonable certainty that this is also the cause of the cold closet in the kitchen. Note: Around other ceiling penetrations in the mechanical room, such as the domestic hot water chimney, you should use high temperature caulking to seal up any gaps. 

** Another large source of air leakage in your home was through the chimney for the hot water tank. Warm air from the basement is constantly rising up through this chimney, regardless of whether water is being heated or not, as the chimney is open to the indoor environment.

** Some of the slider windows (as discussed on site) on the lowest floor are leaky. This is a common problem with slider windows, and a full replacement of the windows is something to be considered. See Window Upgrade section.

** Around the attic hatch in the front closet is a small source of air leakage upstairs. This can be sealed with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack".

Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss, but it could result in a larger reduction. This is estimated to save you $140 per year and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.8 GJ. Although the savings aren't huge, the increase in comfort downstairs will be noticeable, for relatively low effort/ expense.

There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.

An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.
</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>
MAIN WALLS

The main exterior walls of your home, for the purposes of the  modelling, were divided up into variuous types, but basically for your purposes there are two types. 

The first type is what could be considered as the main walls, which are located on the top floor, and the floor with the kitchen and livingroom. The walls in these portions of the house are about 8 inches thick and appear to be of 2x6 construction, spaced at 24" on center, with fibreglass batt insulation in the cavities (R20). Then on  the inside the IR camera showed your walls are "strapped" with what appear to be 2x2s and I don't believe there is insulation in the gap between the drywall and the 2x6 wall (R0). This strapped section allows contractors to run the utilities through here without penetrating the air barrier, making for tightly constructed homes.

The second wall type would be located on any floor that has a portion at, or below grade, so referring to the office and spare room floor, plus the "basement". This wall was about 15 inches thick where I measured, and appears to be 2x6 (R20) on top of the concrete foundation, with an additional 2x6 wall (R20) inside of that, with 2x2 strapping on the interior. For our purposes I have assumed that the additional 2x6 wall was insulated with R20 batts the full height of the wall, making for an R40 nominal wall. AEA doesn't do destructive testing so this is my "best guess" as to the construction.

Since you mentioned that you someday plan to complete a siding upgrade on your home, I have modelled adding 3 additional inches (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to every exterior wall surface, to give you an idea of your energy savings. Due to the large amount of labour involved in adding exterior insulation to a home, and the high cost, this recommendation should only be taken in conjunction with a siding upgrade.

Savings: By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you could potentially save $ 540 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 18 GJ, and qualify for up to a $2,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program insulation rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.
</mainWalls>
                <windows>
WINDOWS

The windows in your house are older double paned, wood framed windows. For their age, they were of good quality as they are low-e coated and were Argon filled. I did see some evidence of blown seals in some of the window units, meaning the original Argon between the panes may no longer be contained within. A few windows in the basement are slider style windows, and I recommend those windows as your priority for an upgrade, as sliders do not seal well. They could be a source of discomfort for your basement.

Upgrading specific windows in this house to ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3 is something that could be considered as an upgrade for this home. If this is something the homeowner wishes to pursue I recommend full frame replacement windows with vinyl frames that are triple pane, double low-E glazed, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22.

Savings: By replacing all 14 windows in the home with ones that meet the criteria described above, you could potentially save $320 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 11 GJ. For the cost of window upgrades, the return on investment is low, however, it may increase comfort in your home, and does contribute to energy savings. If you have any further questions about this pleae feel free to give AEA a call.

There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, however, there are rebates available from the Canada Greener Homes Grant. Please see their website for more details on program requirements.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.
</windows>
                <doors>
DOORS

The three exterioir doors in your home appear to be in good condition. I checked the weather stripping in all locations and it is working as intended, with no immediate repairs necessary. The energy savings on new doors will be minimal and there are currenlty no rebates available from the GNWT for door replacements.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.
</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>
Propane Instantaneous Upgrade from Electric

You currently have a 151 litre conventional propane fired, naturally aspirating domestic hot water heater (approx 2 years old). As discussed during the evaluation of your home, you mentioned it is unlikely that you will upgrade your water heater, but for your interest I thought I would model it as an upgrade for you to consider in the future. If you replace your existing water heater, consider going with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.

In addition to energy savings from burning less propane you will also eliminate a large source of air leakage.

Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, you could potentially save approximately $280 per year in energy costs (10 GJ), and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change from year to year. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.
</HotWaterSystem>
                <Renewables>
SOLAR PV GENERATION:  Hydro Community

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.

Savings: By installing a 5 kW solar photovoltaic system on the south facing roof of your house you could potentially save $1600 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11 GJ. The system size calculated in this report is an estimate, based on you roof size only, and a licenced solar installer should be contacted for a more accurate estimate of the system size that would be ideal for your home.

There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program for electricity generation systems installed in a Hydro Community, including Yellowknife, however there are incentives available from the Canada Greener Homes Program

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a photovoltaic solar panel. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards.
</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>
Your house has one older 13 liter per flush toilet that could be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush!

Savings: By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings. 

There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>
ADDITIONAL COMMENTS

PELLET STOVE

During my site visit it was great to hear that you are using your furnace to circulate heat from the pellet stove. I ran a calculation based estimates that the pellet stove was being used 75% of the time to heat your home, and I calculated that you are currently saving approximately $330 per year to heat your home!

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:

 In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report
 If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.
 Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit
 All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional
 It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:
     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*
     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards
     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home
*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.
 All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change from year to year. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.2 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not a concern for causing backdrafting of your combustion appliances (DHW, pellet stove).

During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:
       
        - two bathroom fans
        - a range hood
        - a clothes dryer
      
Your bathroom fans appeared to seal well, but someday you may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs  Yellowknife

The fuel values below may be different than current prices, as they have been averaged over the past 5 years to account for fuel price fluctuations over time. These prices have been used to calculate energy costs and energy savings in Yellowknife, for your report:

Electricity - $0.32 per kWh
Oil - $1.24 per liter 
Propane - $0.75 per liter delivered
Wood - $410 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $5.79 per bag, $351.29 per tonne bulk delivered


